.\" Manual page of the radiolack command, installed by the Debian package.
.\" packaging/build_deb.py fills in the version and the date.
.TH RADIOLACK 1 "@DATE@" "Radiolack @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
radiolack \- receive\-only SDR receiver with band plans
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B radiolack
[\fB\-\-ui\fR \fIwindow\fR|\fIbrowser\fR|\fInone\fR]
[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR]
[\fB\-\-remote\fR [\fIPORT\fR]]
[\fB\-\-rtl\-sdr\fR [\fIDEVICE\fR] | \fB\-\-rtl\-tcp\fR [\fIADDRESS\fR]]
.br
.B radiolack
\fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Radiolack shows the spectrum and the waterfall of an SDR receiver in a
desktop window, plays NFM, AM, USB, LSB and CW, and lists the signals it
finds, in the receiver's span or across a range that it sweeps.
It only receives: it has no transmit function.
It reads an RTL\-SDR receiver plugged into this computer, or one that an
rtl_tcp server serves from another computer, and labels every frequency
with a simplified European band plan, with the IARU Region\ 1 plans
inside the amateur bands.
.PP
Without \fB\-\-rtl\-sdr\fR or \fB\-\-rtl\-tcp\fR, Radiolack opens the
source used last: the simulated 2\ m band the first time.
The Source panel switches between sources.
Closing the window stops Radiolack; without a window, stop it with
Ctrl+C.
.PP
Radiolack serves its interface on 127.0.0.1 only, and answers only requests
addressed to 127.0.0.1 or localhost.
.PP
Remote access lets phones, tablets and other computers on the local
network control Radiolack and hear it.
It is off at each start, and only this computer turns it on: its
interface, in Settings, or the \fB\-\-remote\fR option.
Radiolack then also listens over HTTPS, on port 8443 unless Settings or
\fB\-\-remote\fR chooses another, on this computer's private IPv4
addresses, and answers only devices on these networks.
A device installs the certificate of Radiolack's local authority, whose
SHA\-256 fingerprint Settings shows, and enters the access code that
Settings shows, once: \fB\-\-remote\fR prints both.
A paired device shares the receiver, but cannot change remote access, the
update check or the rtl_tcp server's address.
Never make remote access's port reachable from the Internet.
.PP
At each start, unless Settings turns the check off, Radiolack asks its APT
repository whether a newer version is out, and says so in its interface:
the system's software updates install it.
The check runs apt as the current user, with Radiolack's APT source alone,
in a temporary folder, and installs nothing.
It is the only connection Radiolack makes besides the receiver and its
interfaces, and it carries no identifier.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-ui\fR \fIwindow\fR|\fIbrowser\fR|\fInone\fR
Show the interface in a desktop window (the default), in the default web
browser, or nowhere: Radiolack then only prints the interface's address.
When the window cannot open, for instance without a graphical display,
Radiolack says why, keeps running and prints the address.
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR
Listen on \fIPORT\fR, on 127.0.0.1, instead of any free port.
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\fR [\fIPORT\fR]
Turn remote access on at start, as Settings does, on \fIPORT\fR, from
1024 to 65535: by default, the port used last, 8443 the first time.
Remote access keeps \fIPORT\fR for the next starts.
Radiolack prints the addresses that devices open, the SHA\-256
fingerprint of its certificate and the access code, which then lands
wherever the output goes.
When remote access cannot turn on, for instance on a port already in use
or without an address on a local network, Radiolack says why and exits
with status 1.
Without a window, Settings stays on 127.0.0.1: reach it through an SSH
tunnel whose two ends use the port given to \fB\-\-port\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-rtl\-sdr\fR [\fIDEVICE\fR]
Read the RTL\-SDR receiver plugged into this computer, through an rtl_tcp
that Radiolack runs while an interface is open.
\fIDEVICE\fR is the receiver's index or serial number: 0, the first one,
by default.
rtl_tcp comes with the rtl\-sdr package.
.TP
\fB\-\-rtl\-tcp\fR [\fIADDRESS\fR]
Read an RTL\-SDR receiver through an rtl_tcp server that you run, at
\fIADDRESS\fR: \fIHOST\fR, \fIPORT\fR or \fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR.
By default, the address used last: 127.0.0.1:1234 the first time.
The Source panel can change it.
Not with \fB\-\-rtl\-sdr\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/radiolack/settings.json\fR
The settings of each receiver, the scanners' settings, the preferences of
this computer's interfaces and whether to check for updates at start.
.TP
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/radiolack/bookmarks.json\fR
The bookmarks.
.TP
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/radiolack/observations.json\fR
The History: the channels where Radiolack found signals in the last
90\ days, apart from the simulated band's, with when, how often and how
long each was heard.
.TP
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/radiolack/remote.json\fR
What remote access keeps: its port, its access code, the paired devices
with their interface preferences, and its local authority.
Remote access is off at each start all the same, unless
\fB\-\-remote\fR turns it on.
.TP
\fI/etc/apt/sources.list.d/radiolack.sources\fR
Radiolack's APT source, which the package adds with the repository's key,
\fI/etc/apt/keyrings/radiolack\-archive\-keyring.gpg\fR.
With \fBEnabled: no\fR, apt no longer uses the repository, and Radiolack no
longer checks for updates.
.PP
Without \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR, or when it is not an absolute path, the
folder is \fI~/.config/radiolack\fR.
A file that Radiolack cannot read is renamed aside, never overwritten, and
Radiolack starts from its defaults.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 once Radiolack stops, after its window closes or on Ctrl+C or SIGTERM;
1 when it cannot start, for instance on a port already in use, or when
\fB\-\-remote\fR cannot turn remote access on;
2 for an invalid option.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rtl_tcp (1)
